"For each item below, fetch its source and check that the recorded quote appears on the page and supports the item. A card item is a field of the plan in the producer's words, with the sources it cites: check each factual claim in it against those sources, and any value it carries against the page; what the plan will build, measure or predict needs no source, but a factual claim with no source is UNSUPPORTED. For a cost input, check that the page states the number for the setting given; whether it applies to this plan is not yours to judge. When the page states a different number for a num or rate item, give it as page_value. Decide only from the pages you fetch and the text below; do not read files. Return one status per item, under its ref: SUPPORTED, PARTIAL, UNSUPPORTED, CONTRADICTED or UNREACHABLE, with the page sentence you relied on.
Items, one per line:
{\"ref\":\"card:question\",\"text\":\"Can computed capsid–receptor structures and pH-resolved MD contact lifetimes classify which AAV capsids cross brain endothelium versus stay inside cells?\",\"sources\":[]}
{\"ref\":\"card:hypothesis\",\"text\":\"Epitope geometry from AlphaFold plus pH contact survival from many short replicas separates transcytosing LRP6/ALPL binders from retained capsids [s_lrp6][s_moyer].\",\"sources\":[{\"id\":\"s_lrp6\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11381518/\",\"quote\":\"Representative AlphaFold models, from 5 structural models each of X1 and CAP-Mac peptides with up to 20 recycles, predict selective interaction with human LRP6 domain E1 (E1 and E2: teal, E3 and E4: yellow, AAV peptides: purple).\"},{\"id\":\"s_moyer\",\"url\":\"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2024.03.12.584703v1\",\"quote\":\"We observed direct capsid-receptor interaction in a cell-free assay using Surface Plasmon Resonance (SPR) analysis with recombinant ALPL, showing that VCAP-102 directly interacts with ALPL with an affinity (KD) of ∼20nm at pH7.4 (Fig. 4c). Rapid dissociation of the ALPL-VCAP-102 complex was observed at pH 5.5.\"}]}
{\"ref\":\"card:sequential_part\",\"text\":\"One trajectory's 10^9–10^12 ordered 2-fs steps, each frame waiting on the last [s_jones].\",\"sources\":[{\"id\":\"s_jones\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9281402/\",\"quote\":\"The number of sequential operations needed to achieve these time scales can grow to be at least 10^9–10^12.\"}]}
{\"ref\":\"card:method\",\"text\":\"Many short independent replicas run at once; contacts decorrelate inside each replica, so weak scaling beats one long run [s_kasson][s_kutzner_ens].\",\"sources\":[{\"id\":\"s_kasson\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9476681/\",\"quote\":\"In a system with multiple metastable states and good separation of timescales, individual trajectories will decorrelate within a metastable state much faster than they transition between states.\"},{\"id\":\"s_kutzner_ens\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/1903.05918v1\",\"quote\":\"problems that previously required the generation of a few long trajectories are nowadays often addressed by ensemble methods that instead need a large number of shorter trajectories.\"}]}
{\"ref\":\"card:if_lever\",\"text\":\"At 32 ten-ns replicas per condition the score reaches AUC ≥ 0.9 and pH ratio > 2, matching the 320-ns run [s_moyer].\",\"sources\":[{\"id\":\"s_moyer\",\"url\":\"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2024.03.12.584703v1\",\"quote\":\"We observed direct capsid-receptor interaction in a cell-free assay using Surface Plasmon Resonance (SPR) analysis with recombinant ALPL, showing that VCAP-102 directly interacts with ALPL with an affinity (KD) of ∼20nm at pH7.4 (Fig. 4c). Rapid dissociation of the ALPL-VCAP-102 complex was observed at pH 5.5.\"}]}
{\"ref\":\"card:if_not\",\"text\":\"At 8–32 replicas the score stays at AUC ≤ 0.75 with no pH difference; the 320-ns run agrees, so more replicas do not help.\",\"sources\":[]}
{\"ref\":\"card:test_summary\",\"text\":\"ColabFold AlphaFold-Multimer on 24 peptide–receptor complexes, then 32 ten-ns GROMACS replicas per pair at pH 7.4 and 5.5 on 8 V100s; compared with 320-ns sequential runs and published binding, domain and pH-release results [s_lrp6][s_moyer].\",\"sources\":[{\"id\":\"s_lrp6\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11381518/\",\"quote\":\"Representative AlphaFold models, from 5 structural models each of X1 and CAP-Mac peptides with up to 20 recycles, predict selective interaction with human LRP6 domain E1 (E1 and E2: teal, E3 and E4: yellow, AAV peptides: purple).\"},{\"id\":\"s_moyer\",\"url\":\"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2024.03.12.584703v1\",\"quote\":\"We observed direct capsid-receptor interaction in a cell-free assay using Surface Plasmon Resonance (SPR) analysis with recombinant ALPL, showing that VCAP-102 directly interacts with ALPL with an affinity (KD) of ∼20nm at pH7.4 (Fig. 4c). Rapid dissociation of the ALPL-VCAP-102 complex was observed at pH 5.5.\"}]}
{\"ref\":\"card:closest_prior\",\"text\":\"Adaptive-sampling Markov models matched one long villin run with up to 5,000 parallel simulations per iteration [s_bowman]; capsid work used 5 AlphaFold models per peptide [s_lrp6].\",\"sources\":[{\"id\":\"s_bowman\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3637129/\",\"quote\":\"Figure 1A shows that the wall-clock time efficiency of adaptive sampling scales linearly up to 5,000 simulations per iteration.\"},{\"id\":\"s_lrp6\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11381518/\",\"quote\":\"Representative AlphaFold models, from 5 structural models each of X1 and CAP-Mac peptides with up to 20 recycles, predict selective interaction with human LRP6 domain E1 (E1 and E2: teal, E3 and E4: yellow, AAV peptides: purple).\"}],\"value\":5000}
{\"ref\":\"card:left_open\",\"text\":\"Whether computed pH-resolved contact lifetimes classify transcytosis versus retention at all; only one pair has measured pH kinetics [s_moyer].\",\"sources\":[{\"id\":\"s_moyer\",\"url\":\"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2024.03.12.584703v1\",\"quote\":\"We observed direct capsid-receptor interaction in a cell-free assay using Surface Plasmon Resonance (SPR) analysis with recombinant ALPL, showing that VCAP-102 directly interacts with ALPL with an affinity (KD) of ∼20nm at pH7.4 (Fig. 4c). Rapid dissociation of the ALPL-VCAP-102 complex was observed at pH 5.5.\"}]}
{\"ref\":\"card:anchor\",\"text\":\"Reproduce the published AlphaFold-Multimer result: capsid peptides X1 and CAP-Mac bind LRP6 domain 1, not domains 3 and 4 [s_lrp6].\",\"sources\":[{\"id\":\"s_lrp6\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11381518/\",\"quote\":\"Representative AlphaFold models, from 5 structural models each of X1 and CAP-Mac peptides with up to 20 recycles, predict selective interaction with human LRP6 domain E1 (E1 and E2: teal, E3 and E4: yellow, AAV peptides: purple).\"}],\"value\":\"E1 preferred over E3E4 for both X1 and CAP-Mac peptides\"}
{\"ref\":\"card:limits\",\"text\":\"Ten-ns contact-survival proxies, not millisecond off-rates. Peptide–domain fragments in water, not full glycosylated capsids. Six pairs, no cell-level transcytosis measured.\",\"sources\":[]}
{\"ref\":\"src:s_lrp6\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11381518/\",\"quote\":\"Representative AlphaFold models, from 5 structural models each of X1 and CAP-Mac peptides with up to 20 recycles, predict selective interaction with human LRP6 domain E1 (E1 and E2: teal, E3 and E4: yellow, AAV peptides: purple).\",\"supports\":\"the method precedent (5 models, 20 recycles), the anchor target (E1 not E3E4), and af_models and af_recycles\"}
{\"ref\":\"src:s_moyer\",\"url\":\"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2024.03.12.584703v1\",\"quote\":\"We observed direct capsid-receptor interaction in a cell-free assay using Surface Plasmon Resonance (SPR) analysis with recombinant ALPL, showing that VCAP-102 directly interacts with ALPL with an affinity (KD) of ∼20nm at pH7.4 (Fig. 4c). Rapid dissociation of the ALPL-VCAP-102 complex was observed at pH 5.5.\",\"supports\":\"the only measured pH-resolved pair the computed pH ratio is scored against (open preprint; the journal version is paywalled)\"}
{\"ref\":\"src:s_bowman\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3637129/\",\"quote\":\"Figure 1A shows that the wall-clock time efficiency of adaptive sampling scales linearly up to 5,000 simulations per iteration.\",\"supports\":\"novelty.closest_prior and prior_max_level: parallel-run scaling up to 5,000 simulations\"}
{\"ref\":\"src:s_jones\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9281402/\",\"quote\":\"The number of sequential operations needed to achieve these time scales can grow to be at least 10^9–10^12.\",\"supports\":\"the sequential part: one MD trajectory is a long ordered chain of integration steps\"}
